frame.situation:
PUBLIC RECORD (bulletin, round 2)
- Our programme: NGSA, launched in 2030 on code 7 with the Alt plan, is in development for entry into service in 2037.
- Engines launched on our selection: P&W GTF2 is ready in 2036 and Rolls-Royce UltraFan NB in 2037, with no margin. CFM is always fitted, and the GEnx upgrade has been ready since 2033.
- Boeing has made no programme move. On the record it has:
  - no fps;
  - no 787 Re-engine;
  - no Rate Increase.
- Boeing's line:
  - the 737 stays at 57 a month;
  - the 787 and 777X lines stay 'the way they are now';
  - on a new single-aisle, 'we're not there yet, and I'm not going to give you a date';
  - 'more important that we do this right than fast';
  - dual engine sources are a requirement for its next narrowbody.
- That points to S1. Two caveats:
  - Boeing's dual-source condition can now be met with GTF2 and UltraFan NB on the market.
  - The 737 MAX phases out from 2044, and Boeing's next chance to launch after this round is 2045.
  - So an fps this round is plausible despite the words. Profile §5 row 15: a rival saying it will not launch is ignored for NGSA timing, and it does not move our clock either way.
- Other makers:
  - Rolls-Royce keeps the Joint Venture open to P&W.
  - P&W competes on standard terms.
  - CFM offers both airframers an engine and keeps RISE (not before 2045) as a technology programme.
- Observed narrowbody deliveries for 2026-2034: Airbus 60.6%, Boeing 39.4% (1,020 against 664 in 2034). Widebody: 41.2%.

OUR POSITION IF NOBODY MOVES AGAIN
- ΔPV +11.62 $B (yield 107.75%). Components:
  - narrowbody operating profit +61.59;
  - programme investment -15.23;
  - debt penalty -0.53;
  - supply and demand -33.87;
  - Alt-plan cost -0.32.
- The brief reports no safety, quality or supply event.

TRANSITION GAP
- In 2039-2040, capacity of 734+93 and 556+296 meets demand of 1,931 and 1,972.
- Preference share falls to 47.0% and 47.4%, and the slack goes to Boeing.
- In the status quo we would deliver 1,020 and 1,030 in those years; on our plan we deliver 827 and 852.

OBJECTIVE (scored by the GM; never changes the payoff)
- Defend 60/40: not met (worst year 2040, 47.4%).
- Protect the A320 family before entry into service: met (60.0%).
- Engine-supply enabler: met (3 sources and 3 makers in 2040, 2045 and 2050).

CASH
- NGSA costs $35.52B / 7 = $5.074B a year in 2035-2036, against one year's FCF of EUR 4,753m x 1.10 = $5.228B [AX-0104].
- Headroom is $0.154B a year.
- The round-1 condition caps Alt-plan cash at about $0.13B a year to 2036, preferably nil.

BOARD GUIDANCE (round 2)
- Risk-averse, 4 of 5. It funds NGSA alone through 2035-2036.
- It would approve:
  - ngsa hold;
  - a320_plan alt kept;
  - code 7 kept;
  - rea350 hold;
  - Delay Tactics none.
- It would veto:
  - rea350 launch, on balance-sheet grounds;
  - any Delay Tactics: naked, and bottleneck or both always;
  - an NGSA cancel;
  - a switch to the base plan;
  - any item Wagner fails on safety, quality, ramp shield or supply crunch;
  - an equity raise or a dividend cut outside a crisis;
  - any item losing more than $2B in any column.
- One round-1 point is outstanding. The published text kept 'matured over the past decade'; the Board asked for 'a technology and production system we have been maturing'.

red_lines:
