board_guidance.expect_to_see:
The Board expects the package to contain the following. Every column (S1-S4) counts as plausible, because the bulletin rules none out.

1. Grid rows. The submitted row and the row with each Board item at `hold`, column by column. For commit with GTF2 at hold, the rows are hold/commit -1.53 / -16.22 / +0.40 / +0.68 and hold/hold -1.53 / -16.22 / +0.40 / -1.53. Bring the cancel rows as well, even if cancel is not ordered.

2. From the CEO: his expected scenario, `predictions` over S1-S4 and `premium_b`. We expect a premium of 0, since commit is worth at least 0 under any weights. We also expect his response to each Board recommendation in round 1.

3. From Mitchill:
- his column probabilities, one governing set, as asked in round 1;
- commit's weighted item value (+2.21 x p(S4); +0.55 uniform);
- his 2035 cancel rule applied (carry, because NGSA's code includes P&W) and the $1.67B write-off for reference;
- fold arithmetic: $1.67B spent, against a $2B share, so $0 written off and no new R&D;
- 2030-35 GTF2 cash by year beside the dividend and the path back to pre-ASR debt;
- his four-ground veto check, with ground 3 applied to the Joint Venture terms.

4. From Eddy:
- GTF2's ready year (2036) against NGSA EIS 2037, and against an fps 10-yr EIS of 2045 in S3;
- the Joint Venture engine's ready year if it forms in 2035 (2036 with GTF2 folded, 2041 without);
- the durability campaign against plan, with core and gear on test, all life-limited parts at full life at EIS, and testing not compressed;
- his parts and capacity result for the GTF fleet and a GTF2 production ramp gated on spares;
- his veto check on his two grounds, including his three round-1 triggers (quality escape or rising AOG, compressed testing, durability not provable by 2037).

